==English==
{{wikipedia|Trooper (rank)}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|troop|er|lang=en}}, from {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|troupe}}.

Sense of cavalry soldier attested 1640, mounted policeman 1858 (Australia), state policeman 1911 (US). The sense of "one who endures adversity" comes from English {{m|en|trouper||member of an acting troupe}}, 1959, but through assimilation with the sense of "soldier" has come to be usually spelled "trooper".

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈtɹuːpə(ɹ)/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|uːpə(ɹ)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|military}} A soldier of private rank in [[cavalry]] or [[armour]]. Abbreviated [[Tpr]].
# A [[cavalry]] horse; [[charger]].
# A [[soldier]].
# {{lb|en|British}} A [[troopship]].
# {{lb|en|US}} A [[state trooper]].
# {{lb|en|Australia}} A [[mounted]] [[policeman]].
# One who endures adversity or hardship with an attitude of [[stoicism]] and [[persistence]].
#: ''He was a real '''trooper''' about taking care of the kids for the weekend.''

